<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- MATH 14 - Review Problems #4, the version WITH solutions.               -->
<!--                                                                        -->
<!-- Each problem is a <problem> (an EXAMPLE-LIKE block) whose <solution>    -->
<!-- is printed immediately below it: no answer boxes, no "Check me" and no  -->
<!-- "Randomize".  The randomised parameters of the practice version have    -->
<!-- been fixed at values chosen to keep every answer clean.  The auto-      -->
<!-- graded version of the same sixteen problems is review4-practice.ptx.    -->
<!--                                                                        -->
<!-- HIDING THE SOLUTIONS.  Every <solution> here carries                    -->
<!--   component="review4-solutions"                                        -->
<!-- so the whole set can be switched off from publication/publication.ptx   -->
<!-- without touching this file; see the comment in <source> there.          -->
<section xml:id="sec-exercises-review4-online" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Review Problems #4</title>

  <p>
    Sections: <xref ref="section-greens-theorem" text="title"/>,
    <xref ref="section-surface-integrals" text="title"/>, and
    <xref ref="section-divergence-theorem" text="title"/>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Work each problem yourself first; the full solution follows immediately
    below it. The same sixteen problems, with the numbers randomised and
    every answer checked automatically, are in
    <xref ref="sec-exercises-review4-practice" text="title"/>.
  </p>

  <problem xml:id="ex-r4o-box-flux">
    <title>Outward Flux Through the Surface of a Box</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
      Evaluate the <alert>outward</alert> flux of the vector field
      </p>
      <me>
      \mathbf{F} = 3x^2\,\mathbf{i} + z^4\,\mathbf{j} + e^z\,\mathbf{k}
      </me>
      <p>
      through the surface <m>S</m> of the solid box <m>W</m> given by
      <m>0 \le x \le 2</m>, <m>0 \le y \le 3</m>, <m>0 \le z \le 1</m>. Set up
      the triple integral that the Divergence Theorem provides, and evaluate it.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="review4-solutions">
      <p>
        By the Divergence Theorem,
        <m>\displaystyle\iint_S \mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma
        = \iiint_W \nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}\,dV</m> with
        <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = 2Ax + e^z</m>, where <m>A</m> is the
        coefficient of <m>x^2</m>. Writing the triple integral in the order
        <m>dx\,dy\,dz</m>, the limits are the <m>z</m>-limits outside, then
        the <m>y</m>-limits, then the <m>x</m>-limits inside. Integrating,
      </p>
      <me>
        \text{Flux} = A p^2 q s + p q \left(e^{s} - 1\right),
      </me>
      <p>
        where <m>p, q, s</m> are the <m>x</m>-, <m>y</m>- and
        <m>z</m>-dimensions of the box.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here <m>A = 3</m> and the box is <m>2 \times 3 \times 1</m>, so the flux is
<m>3\left(2^2\right)(3)(1) + (2)(3)\left(e - 1\right) = 30 + 6e \approx 46.31</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </problem>
  <problem xml:id="ex-r4o-box-div-max">
    <title>The Box With the Largest Flux</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
      Let <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = 4\left(4 - x\right)</m> and
      <m>0 \le a, b, c \le 12</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
      (a) Find the flux of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> out of the rectangular solid
      <m>0 \le x \le a</m>, <m>0 \le y \le b</m>, <m>0 \le z \le c</m>, as an
      expression in <m>a</m>, <m>b</m> and <m>c</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
      (b) For which values of <m>a</m>, <m>b</m> and <m>c</m> is that flux
      largest, and what is the largest flux?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="review4-solutions">
      <p>
        By the Divergence Theorem the flux out of the box is
        <m>\displaystyle\int_0^{c}\!\!\int_0^{b}\!\!\int_0^{a}
        2k\left(A - x\right)dx\,dy\,dz = k\left(2Aa - a^2\right)bc</m>,
        where <m>2k</m> is the leading coefficient and <m>A</m> the root of
        the divergence. Since <m>b</m> and <m>c</m> multiply a positive
        quantity, take <m>b = c = M</m>, the largest allowed value; and
        <m>2Aa - a^2</m> is maximized at <m>a = A</m>. The largest flux is
        <m>k A^2 M^2</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here <m>2k = 4</m>, <m>A = 4</m> and <m>M = 12</m>, so the flux is
<m>2\left(8a - a^2\right)bc</m>, largest at <m>a = 4</m>, <m>b = c = 12</m>,
where it equals <m>2\left(4^2\right)\left(12^2\right) = 4608</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </problem>
  <problem xml:id="ex-r4o-verify-cylinder">
    <title>Verifying the Divergence Theorem on a Cylinder</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
      Verify the Divergence Theorem for the vector field
      </p>
      <me>
      \mathbf{F} = \langle 3x,\ 4z,\ 5y \rangle
      </me>
      <p>
      and the region <m>x^2 + y^2 \le 1</m>, <m>0 \le z \le 2</m>: compute the
      flux <m>\iint_S \mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma</m> across the
      closed surface directly, compute
      <m>\iiint_W \nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}\,dV</m> over the solid, and check
      that the two agree.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="review4-solutions">
      <p>
        With <m>\mathbf{F} = \langle Ax, Bz, Cy\rangle</m> we get
        <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = A</m>, so
        <m>\iiint_W \nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}\,dV
        = A\,\text{Vol}(W) = A\pi h</m>. Computing the surface integral
        directly: on the side, <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}
        = A\cos^2\theta + Bz\sin\theta</m> integrates to <m>A h\pi</m>; the
        top and bottom disks contribute <m>0</m> by symmetry, since there
        <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}} = \pm Cy</m>. Both sides equal
        <m>A h \pi</m>, verifying the theorem.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here <m>A = 3</m> and <m>h = 2</m>, so both sides equal
<m>3(2)\pi = 6\pi \approx 18.85</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </problem>
  <problem xml:id="ex-r4o-sphere">
    <title>Flux Through a Sphere</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
      Use the Divergence Theorem to evaluate the surface integral
      <m>\displaystyle\iint_{S} \mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma</m>,
      where
      </p>
      <me>
      \mathbf{F} = \langle x^3,\ 4,\ z^3 \rangle
      </me>
      <p>
      and <m>S</m> is the sphere <m>x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 9</m>, oriented outward.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="review4-solutions">
      <p>
        <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = 3x^2 + 3z^2</m>. By symmetry of the
        ball <m>W</m> of radius <m>R</m>, the integrals of <m>x^2</m>,
        <m>y^2</m>, <m>z^2</m> over <m>W</m> are all equal to
        <m>\frac13\iiint_W \rho^2\,dV
        = \frac13\int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{\pi}\!\!\int_0^{R}
        \rho^4\sin\phi\,d\rho\,d\phi\,d\theta = \frac{4\pi R^5}{15}</m>.
        Hence
      </p>
      <me>
        \iint_S \mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma
        = \iiint_W 3\left(x^2 + z^2\right)dV
        = 6\cdot\frac{4\pi R^5}{15} = \frac{8\pi R^5}{5}.
      </me>
      <p>
        Here <m>R = 3</m>, so the flux is
<m>\dfrac{8\pi\left(3^5\right)}{5} = \dfrac{1944\pi}{5} \approx 1221.45</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </problem>
  <problem xml:id="ex-r4o-paraboloid-cap">
    <title>Flux Through a Paraboloid Cap</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
      Let
      <m>\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = z\tan\!\left(y^2\right)\mathbf{i}
      + z^3\ln\!\left(x^2+1\right)\mathbf{j} + 3z\,\mathbf{k}</m>. Use the
      Divergence Theorem to find the flux of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> across the part
      of the paraboloid <m>x^2 + y^2 + z = 5</m> that lies above the plane
      <m>z = 1</m> and is oriented upward.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="review4-solutions">
      <p>
        Write <m>b</m> for the coefficient of <m>z\,\mathbf{k}</m> and
        <m>a^2 = h - 1</m>, so the paraboloid meets the plane <m>z=1</m>
        in the circle of radius <m>a</m>. Then
        <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = b</m>. Close the surface with the
        disk <m>S_1</m> of radius <m>a</m> at <m>z = 1</m>, oriented
        downward; there <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot(-\mathbf{k}) = -b</m>, so
        <m>\iint_{S_1} = -b\pi a^2</m>. The enclosed volume is
        <m>\int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{a}\left(a^2 - r^2\right)r\,dr\,d\theta
        = \tfrac{\pi a^4}{2}</m>, so the closed-surface flux is
        <m>b\,\tfrac{\pi a^4}{2}</m>, and
      </p>
      <me>
        \iint_S \mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma
        = b\,\frac{\pi a^4}{2} - \left(-b\pi a^2\right)
        = b\left(\frac{a^4}{2} + a^2\right)\pi.
      </me>
      <p>
        Here <m>b = 3</m> and <m>a^2 = 5 - 1 = 4</m>, so the flux is
<m>3\left(\tfrac{2^4}{2} + 2^2\right)\pi = 36\pi \approx 113.10</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </problem>
  <problem xml:id="ex-r4o-cylinder-x-axis">
    <title>Radial Field Through a Closed Cylinder</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
      Compute the flux of the vector field
      <m>\mathbf{F} = x\,\mathbf{i} + y\,\mathbf{j} + z\,\mathbf{k}</m>
      through the surface <m>S</m>, which is a closed cylinder of radius
      <m>3</m> centered on the <m>x</m>-axis, with <m>-2 \le x \le 2</m>, and
      oriented outward.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="review4-solutions">
      <p>
        The surface is closed, so by the Divergence Theorem with
        <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = 3</m>,
        <m>\text{Flux} = 3\,\text{Vol}(W) = 3\,\pi R^2 (2L) = 6\pi R^2 L</m>,
        where <m>R</m> is the radius and <m>2L</m> the length of the
        cylinder.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here <m>R = 3</m> and <m>L = 2</m>, so the flux is
<m>6\pi\left(3^2\right)(2) = 108\pi \approx 339.29</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </problem>
  <problem xml:id="ex-r4o-helicoid">
    <title>Flux Through a Helicoid</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
      Evaluate
      <m>\displaystyle\iint_S \mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma</m>,
      where the vector field is
      <m>\mathbf{F} = \mathbf{i} + \mathbf{j} + \mathbf{k}</m> and the
      parametrized surface <m>S</m> is the helicoid
      </p>
      <me>
      \mathbf{r}(u,v) = \left(v\cos u,\ v\sin u,\ u\right),
      \qquad 0 \le u \le \pi, \quad 0 \le v \le 1,
      </me>
      <p>
      oriented upward.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="review4-solutions">
      <p>
        With <m>\mathbf{r}_u = \left(-v\sin u, v\cos u, 1\right)</m> and
        <m>\mathbf{r}_v = \left(\cos u, \sin u, 0\right)</m>, the upward
        normal is
        <m>\mathbf{r}_v\times\mathbf{r}_u
        = \sin u\,\mathbf{i} - \cos u\,\mathbf{j} + v\,\mathbf{k}</m>.
        Writing <m>c</m> for the common component of <m>\mathbf{F}</m>,
      </p>
      <me>
        \iint_S \mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma
        = c\int_0^1\!\!\int_0^{\pi}\left(\sin u - \cos u + v\right)
        du\,dv = c\left(2 + \frac{\pi}{2}\right).
      </me>
      <p>
        Here <m>c = 1</m>, so the flux is
<m>2 + \dfrac{\pi}{2} \approx 3.57</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </problem>
  <problem xml:id="ex-r4o-paraboloid-updisk">
    <title>Flux Through a Paraboloid, Directly</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
      Compute the flux of the vector field
      <m>\mathbf{F} = 2x\,\mathbf{i} + 2y\,\mathbf{j}</m> through the surface
      <m>S</m>, which is the part of the surface
      <m>z = 9 - \left(x^2 + y^2\right)</m> above the disk of radius <m>3</m>
      centered at the origin, oriented <alert>upward</alert>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="review4-solutions">
      <p>
        Writing the surface as <m>z = f(x,y) = R^2 - x^2 - y^2</m> over
        the disk <m>D</m> of radius <m>R</m>,
        <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma = \left(2x\,\mathbf{i} + 2y\,\mathbf{j} +
        \mathbf{k}\right)dx\,dy</m> and
        <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma = 2a\left(x^2+y^2\right)dx\,dy</m>,
        where <m>a</m> is the coefficient in <m>\mathbf{F}</m>. In polar
        coordinates,
      </p>
      <me>
        \text{Flux} = \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{R} 2a\,r^3\,dr\,d\theta
        = \pi a R^4.
      </me>
      <p>
        Here <m>a = 2</m> and <m>R = 3</m>, so the flux is
<m>\pi(2)\left(3^4\right) = 162\pi \approx 508.94</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </problem>
  <figure xml:id="fig-r4o-parallelogram">
    <caption>
      The parallelogram of <xref ref="ex-r4o-parallelogram"/>, with its
      orientation. Note the direction of the arrows.
    </caption>
    <image width="55%">
      <shortdescription>
      A parallelogram anchored at the origin with arrows showing its boundary orientation.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-r4o-parallelogram">
        <diagram dimensions="(360,240)" margins="10">
          <coordinates bbox="(-0.8,-0.55,4.9,2.3)">
            <axes at="pg-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
            <polygon at="pg-par" closed="yes" stroke="#CC0079" thickness="3"
                fill="#CC0079" fill-opacity="0.15"
                points="((0,0),(2,1.4),(4,1.4),(2,0))"/>
            <vector at="pg-e1" v="(0.7,0.49)" tail="(0.75,0.525)"
                stroke="#CC0079" thickness="3"/>
            <vector at="pg-e2" v="(0.8,0)" tail="(2.7,1.4)"
                stroke="#CC0079" thickness="3"/>
            <vector at="pg-e3" v="(-0.7,-0.49)" tail="(3.35,0.945)"
                stroke="#CC0079" thickness="3"/>
            <vector at="pg-e4" v="(-0.8,0)" tail="(1.3,0)"
                stroke="#CC0079" thickness="3"/>
            <point at="pg-O" p="(0,0)" fill="black" alignment="sw">
              <m>(0,0)</m>
            </point>
            <point at="pg-B" p="(2,0)" fill="black" alignment="se">
              <m>(x_0,0)</m>
            </point>
            <point at="pg-T1" p="(2,1.4)" fill="black" alignment="nw">
              <m>(x_0,y_0)</m>
            </point>
            <point at="pg-T2" p="(4,1.4)" fill="black" alignment="ne">
              <m>(2x_0,y_0)</m>
            </point>
          </coordinates>

          <annotations>
            <annotation ref="figure" text="A parallelogram with vertices at the origin, at x nought comma zero, at two x nought comma y nought, and at x nought comma y nought. Arrows on the edges show the boundary traversed clockwise: up the left edge from the origin, rightward across the top, down the right edge, and leftward along the bottom back to the origin.">
              <annotation ref="pg-par" text="The parallelogram with vertices at the origin, x nought comma zero, two x nought comma y nought, and x nought comma y nought."/>
              <annotation ref="pg-e1" text="An arrow on the left edge pointing up and to the right, from the origin toward x nought comma y nought."/>
              <annotation ref="pg-e2" text="An arrow on the top edge pointing to the right."/>
              <annotation ref="pg-e3" text="An arrow on the right edge pointing down and to the left, toward x nought comma zero."/>
              <annotation ref="pg-e4" text="An arrow on the bottom edge pointing to the left, back toward the origin. Altogether the circulation is clockwise."/>
            </annotation>
          </annotations>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
  </figure>
  <problem xml:id="ex-r4o-parallelogram">
    <title>Work Around a Parallelogram</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
      Use Green's Theorem to evaluate the work done by
      <m>\mathbf{F} = \langle x^{3},\ 5x \rangle</m> in moving an object around
      the boundary of the parallelogram in
      <xref ref="fig-r4o-parallelogram"/>
      (<alert>note the orientation</alert>), with <m>x_0 = 3</m> and
      <m>y_0 = 2</m>. Assume that the object circulates only once around the
      boundary, and that its motion begins at the origin and ends at the same
      point.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="review4-solutions">
      <p>
        With <m>M = x^{n}</m> and <m>N = Ax</m>,
        <m>N_x - M_y = A</m>. For the counterclockwise orientation
        Green's Theorem would give <m>A\cdot\text{Area} = A x_0 y_0</m>;
        the boundary in the figure is traversed clockwise, so
        <m>\text{Work} = -A x_0 y_0</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here <m>A = 5</m>, <m>x_0 = 3</m> and <m>y_0 = 2</m>, so the work is
<m>-5(3)(2) = -30</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </problem>
  <problem xml:id="ex-r4o-three-fields">
    <title>Three Gradient Fields and the Unit Circle</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
      (a) Each of the vector fields
      </p>
      <md>
      <mrow>
      \mathbf{F} \amp = 2y\,\mathbf{i} + 2x\,\mathbf{j},
      </mrow>
      <mrow>
      \mathbf{G} \amp = \frac{3y}{x^2+y^2}\,\mathbf{i}
        + \frac{-3x}{x^2+y^2}\,\mathbf{j},
      </mrow>
      <mrow>
      \mathbf{H} \amp = \frac{4x}{\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}\,\mathbf{i}
        + \frac{4y}{\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}\,\mathbf{j}
      </mrow>
      </md>
      <p>
      is a gradient vector field on some domain (not necessarily the whole
      plane). Find potential functions for <m>\mathbf{F}</m> and
      <m>\mathbf{H}</m>, choosing the constant so that the potential is
      <m>0</m> at the origin, and find the constant <m>C</m> for which
      <m>g(x,y) = C\arctan\!\left(\dfrac{x}{y}\right)</m> is a potential
      function for <m>\mathbf{G}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
      (b) Find the line integrals of <m>\mathbf{F}</m>, <m>\mathbf{G}</m> and
      <m>\mathbf{H}</m> around the curve <m>C</m>, the unit circle in the
      <m>xy</m>-plane centered at the origin and traversed counterclockwise.
      </p>
      <p>
      (c) For which of the three vector fields can Green's Theorem be used to
      calculate the line integral in part (b), and why?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="review4-solutions">
      <p>
        (a) <m>f(x,y) = a_1 xy</m>,
        <m>g(x,y) = a_2\arctan\!\left(x/y\right)</m> (so <m>C = a_2</m>,
        valid where <m>y \ne 0</m>), and
        <m>h(x,y) = a_3\sqrt{x^2+y^2}</m> (valid except at the origin),
        where <m>a_1, a_2, a_3</m> are the coefficients in
        <m>\mathbf{F}</m>, <m>\mathbf{G}</m>, <m>\mathbf{H}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (b) <m>\int_C \mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r} = 0</m> since
        <m>\mathbf{F}</m> is a gradient field on the whole plane and
        <m>C</m> is closed. On the unit circle <m>\mathbf{G}</m> is
        tangent, of length <m>a_2</m>, pointing clockwise, so
        <m>\int_C \mathbf{G}\cdot d\mathbf{r} = -2\pi a_2</m>.
        <m>\mathbf{H}</m> is radial, hence perpendicular to <m>C</m>, so
        its integral is <m>0</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (c) Only <m>\mathbf{F}</m>: the domains of <m>\mathbf{G}</m> and
        <m>\mathbf{H}</m> do not include the origin, which is inside
        <m>C</m>, so Green's Theorem does not apply to them.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here <m>a_1 = 2</m>, <m>a_2 = 3</m> and <m>a_3 = 4</m>, so
<m>f = 2xy</m>, <m>C = 3</m>, <m>h = 4\sqrt{x^2+y^2}</m>, and the three
line integrals are <m>0</m>, <m>-6\pi \approx -18.85</m>, and <m>0</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </problem>
  <problem xml:id="ex-r4o-rect-circulation">
    <title>Circulation Around a Rectangle</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
      Use Green's Theorem to calculate the circulation of
      <m>\mathbf{F} = 2xy\,\mathbf{i}</m> around the rectangle
      <m>0 \le x \le 4</m>, <m>0 \le y \le 3</m>, oriented counterclockwise.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="review4-solutions">
      <p>
        With <m>M = axy</m> and <m>N = 0</m>,
        <m>N_x - M_y = -ax</m>, so by Green's Theorem
      </p>
      <me>
        \oint_C \mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r}
        = \int_0^{x_0}\!\!\int_0^{y_0} (-ax)\,dy\,dx
        = -\frac{a\,x_0^2\,y_0}{2}.
      </me>
      <p>
        Here <m>a = 2</m>, <m>x_0 = 4</m> and <m>y_0 = 3</m>, so the circulation is
<m>-\dfrac{2\left(4^2\right)(3)}{2} = -48</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </problem>
  <problem xml:id="ex-r4o-circle-green">
    <title>Circulation Around a Circle</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
      Use Green's Theorem to calculate
      </p>
      <me>
      \int_C \left(\left(3x + 4y\right)\mathbf{i}
      + \left(7x + 5y\right)\mathbf{j}\right)\cdot d\mathbf{r},
      </me>
      <p>
      where <m>C</m> is the circular path with center <m>(a,b)</m> and radius
      <m>3</m>, oriented counterclockwise.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="review4-solutions">
      <p>
        <m>N_x - M_y</m> is the constant difference of the two cross
        coefficients, so Green's Theorem gives
        <m>\left(N_x - M_y\right)\cdot\pi m^2</m>, where <m>m</m> is the
        radius; the center <m>(a,b)</m> plays no role.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here <m>N_x - M_y = 7 - 4 = 3</m> and <m>m = 3</m>, so the integral is
<m>3\pi\left(3^2\right) = 27\pi \approx 84.82</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </problem>
  <problem xml:id="ex-r4o-radial-power">
    <title>Divergence of a Family of Radial Fields</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
      Let <m>r = \left(x^2+y^2\right)^{1/2}</m> and consider the vector field
      <m>\mathbf{F} = r^A\!\left(x\,\mathbf{i} + y\,\mathbf{j}\right)</m>,
      where <m>r \ne 0</m> and <m>A</m> is a constant. Note that
      <m>\mathbf{F}</m> has no <m>z</m>-component and is independent of
      <m>z</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
      (a) Find
      <m>\nabla \cdot \left(r^A\!\left(x\,\mathbf{i}
      + y\,\mathbf{j}\right)\right)</m>, and show that it can be written in the
      form <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = c\,r^{a}</m> for constants <m>c</m> and
      <m>a</m> depending on <m>A</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
      (b) For what values of <m>A</m> is <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}</m>
      positive, negative, or zero?
      </p>
      <p>
      (c) What is the sign of the flux out of a small sphere centered at
      <m>(1,1,1)</m> when <m>A = 2</m>, and when <m>A = -5</m>? How would your
      answers change for a small sphere centered at the origin?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="review4-solutions">
      <p>
        (a) With <m>r_x = x/r</m> and <m>r_y = y/r</m>, the product rule
        gives <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = 2r^A + A r^{A-1}
        \left(\tfrac{x^2+y^2}{r}\right) = (2+A)\,r^A</m>, so
        <m>c = 2+A</m> and <m>a = A</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (b) Since <m>r^A \gt 0</m>, the divergence is positive for
        <m>A \gt -2</m>, negative for <m>A \lt -2</m>, and zero for
        <m>A = -2</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (c) The flux out of a small sphere has the sign of the divergence
        at its center, provided the sphere avoids the origin, where the
        field is undefined: positive when <m>A \gt -2</m>, negative when
        <m>A \lt -2</m>. For a sphere centered at the origin the
        calculation above says nothing, since the divergence is not
        defined there.
      </p>
      <p>
        For <m>A = 2 \gt -2</m> the flux out of a small sphere at <m>(1,1,1)</m> is
positive; for <m>A = -5 \lt -2</m> it is negative.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </problem>
  <problem xml:id="ex-r4o-cylinder-band">
    <title>Flux Through a Slanted Cylinder Band</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
      Compute the flux of
      <m>\mathbf{F} = x\,\mathbf{i} + y\,\mathbf{j} + z\,\mathbf{k}</m> through
      the surface of the cylinder <m>x^2 + y^2 = 4</m> bounded below by the
      plane <m>x + y + z = 1</m>, above by the plane <m>x + y + z = 4</m>, and
      <alert>oriented away from the <m>z</m>-axis</alert>. Note that we are
      only interested in the flux through the curved surface of the cylinder.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="review4-solutions">
      <p>
        On the cylinder of radius <m>R</m> the outward normal is
        <m>\langle x, y, 0\rangle / R</m>, so
        <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}} = \left(x^2+y^2\right)/R = R</m>: the
        <m>z</m>-component of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> is tangent to the surface.
        The two slanted planes cut every vertical line on the cylinder in
        a segment of the same length <m>\Delta z = z_1 - z_0</m>, so the
        band has area <m>2\pi R\,\Delta z</m>, and
      </p>
      <me>
        \text{Flux} = R\cdot 2\pi R\,\Delta z = 2\pi R^2\,\Delta z.
      </me>
      <p>
        Here <m>R = 2</m> and <m>\Delta z = 4 - 1 = 3</m>, so the flux is
<m>2\pi\left(2^2\right)(3) = 24\pi \approx 75.40</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </problem>
  <problem xml:id="ex-r4o-cone">
    <title>Flux Out of a Closed Cone</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
      Find the flux of
      <m>\mathbf{F} = 2z\,\mathbf{i} + 3y^2\,\mathbf{j} + 4x\,\mathbf{k}</m>
      out of the closed cone <m>y = \sqrt{x^2 + z^2}</m>, with
      <m>0 \le y \le 2</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="review4-solutions">
      <p>
        <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = 2ay</m>, where <m>a</m> is the
        coefficient of <m>y^2</m>. By the Divergence Theorem, using
        cylindrical coordinates around the <m>y</m>-axis (the cone is
        <m>0 \le \rho \le y \le r</m>),
      </p>
      <me>
        \text{flux} = \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{r}\!\!\int_0^{y}
        2ay\,\rho\,d\rho\,dy\,d\theta
        = 2\pi\int_0^{r} a y^3\,dy = \frac{a r^4 \pi}{2}.
      </me>
      <p>
        Here <m>a = 3</m> and <m>r = 2</m>, so the flux is
<m>\dfrac{3\left(2^4\right)\pi}{2} = 24\pi \approx 75.40</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </problem>
  <problem xml:id="ex-r4o-tetrahedron">
    <title>Flux Through the Surface of a Tetrahedron</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
      Use the Divergence Theorem to calculate the flux of <m>\mathbf{F}</m>
      across <m>S</m>, where
      <m>\mathbf{F} = z\,\mathbf{i} + y\,\mathbf{j} + zx\,\mathbf{k}</m> and
      <m>S</m> is the surface of the tetrahedron enclosed by the coordinate
      planes and the plane
      </p>
      <me>
      \frac{x}{3} + \frac{y}{2} + \frac{z}{4} = 1.
      </me>
    </statement>
    <solution component="review4-solutions">
      <p>
        <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = 0 + 1 + x = 1 + x</m>. The
        tetrahedron <m>E</m> has vertices <m>(0,0,0)</m>,
        <m>(a,0,0)</m>, <m>(0,b,0)</m>, <m>(0,0,c)</m>, so
      </p>
      <me>
        \iint_S \mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma
        = \iiint_E (1+x)\,dV
        = \frac{bc}{2}\int_0^{a}(1+x)\left(1-\frac{x}{a}\right)^2 dx
        = \frac{abc\,(a+4)}{24},
      </me>
      <p>
        using <m>\int_0^{a}(1+x)\left(1-\tfrac{x}{a}\right)^2 dx
        = \tfrac{a}{3} + \tfrac{a^2}{12} = \tfrac{a(a+4)}{12}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here <m>a = 3</m>, <m>b = 2</m> and <m>c = 4</m>, so the flux is
<m>\dfrac{(3)(2)(4)(3+4)}{24} = 7</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </problem>
</section>
